-- Passage rg9-story-details-inference-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('df75738c-4700-5fb4-9027-bc4da494a885', 'rg9-story-details-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Loon Count$q$, $q$The lake survey asked for one number: how many adult loons were on Silver Pond at seven in the morning on the first Saturday of July. Mila had done the count every summer since she was fourteen. This year she was leaving for college in August, and she had decided, without asking, that her younger sister Lucia would take it over.

Lucia did not want it. She wanted to sleep until nine, and she said so twice while Mila loaded the kayak onto the car.

On the water, Mila handed her the binoculars and the data sheet and then simply stopped paddling. "You count," she said. "I'll steer."

The pond was flat and silver, as if someone had ironed it. Lucia found the first pair of loons near the north shore, diving and coming up far from where they had gone down. She lost them twice and had to start over. Then she spotted a third loon alone near the reeds, and a fourth that turned out to be a floating log. Mila said nothing about the log. She only waited while Lucia crossed it out.

Near the inlet, Lucia saw something Mila had never recorded: a small gray chick riding on its parent's back. She held the binoculars up so long that her arms ached. The data sheet had no box for chicks, so she wrote it in the margin and drew an arrow to it.

When they got home, Lucia went straight to the computer to look up how long loon chicks ride on their parents. Mila carried the kayak to the garage by herself. Later, when their mother asked how it went, Mila said the count was fine. Lucia was already asking whether they could go back next Saturday, just to check on the chick.$q$, 'draft')
